Date: Sat, 10 Jul 93 10:29:06 PDT
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: Technical Nanosystems criticisms

My impression of James Donald's arguments about static friction can
be shown by a simple diagram.  Consider two concentric rings of objects
which attract each other:

                                      N
                            N         S          N

                           S                      S
                       N                               N
                        
                        
                       N  S                         S  N

                                              
                            N   S            S   N
                                      N

This is supposed to represent an outer ring of ten N's and an inner ring
of seven S's which is designed to rotate freely.  The S's and N's attract
each other.  In James' discussion, this could be through Van der Waals
forces, but I was thinking of south and north poles of magnets, which
would allow a simple mechanical model of this device to be built.

If the two rings are rigid, then the inner ring should rotate fairly freely.
As it turns, some S's are moving away from N's, but others are moving toward
N's.  These forces should average out and produce a fairly smooth potential
energy curve as a function of rotational position.  There will be little
static friction.

However, if the rings are flexible, a very different situation will occur.
The S's will pull over towards the N's, something like this:

                                      N
                            N         S          N
                             S                  S  

                       N                               N
                        
                        S                            S
                       N                               N

                             S                  S
                            N                    N
                                      N

Now, any attempt to rotate the ring, even by a fairly small amount, will
have to overcome a considerable energy barrier.  The S's will have to "snap"
away from the N's they are straining towards, into a new strained position
at the next N.  The whole process of rotation, even very slow rotation,
will be a constant tug-and-snap, dissipating energy as vibrations of the
rings.  There will be large amounts of static friction, even in the limit
of slow rotation.

I may be misunderstanding James' arguments, but this does seem to be
a mechanism in which large amounts of static friction could arise even
in bearings like those Drexler describes, which if rigid would have smooth
potential energy curves.

Hal Finney
hfinney@shell.portal.com

