Date: Sat, 10 Jul 93 10:29:07 PDT
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: AIT VirtSem: Determinism, Chaos, and These Ideas

Tim writes:

> My contention is that most "logically deep" things appear to have
> high algorithmic information content BECAUSE WE CANNOT DISCOVER THE
> SIMPLE BITS that when run through our "machine" produce the object.
> 
> For example, a screenshot of some unindentified part of the Mandelbrot
> set, or some generation and region of a game of Life, may have a
> simple description, but we have almost no hope of finding it (the
> simple description would be the initial starting pattern, the rules of
> the game (as in Life), the number of generations, and the region).
> Thus, we can do no better than simply describing the object by
> enumerating its precise properties, that is, by listing the 10,000-bit
> string, or the 640 x 480 Mandelbrot set image, or the Life screen.
> 
> Thus, these "purely deterministic" objects are nevertheless
> incompressible.

This is an interesting point.  I imagine that if a scientist or mathematician
of the 1960's were shown an image of a piece of the Mandelbrot set, he
would not have guessed how simple the equation was to produce that picture.
He might instead have expected it to inevitably be the result of some very
complex equation.

Still, though, I think it is useful and important to distinguish between
objects which are logically deep but algorithmically small, and those which
are truly algorithmically large.  There are really two subclasses of the
first group: objects which we don't know are algorithmically small, which
we think are random but which actually have a simple explanation; and those
which we are pretty sure are algorithmically small but for which we can't
easily find the compressed form.

The Mandelbrot set in the 1960's might fall into the first category, and
perhaps turbulence could be said to fall into the second category.

I do think that the distinction between apparently-random and truly-random
phenomena is very important.  It basically separates those things which we
can hope for good theories about from those for which we just have to say,
well, that's just the way it is, there's no good explanation.

So while Tim is right that we may not be able to come up with a good
algorithmic compression of something, we shouldn't blur the distinction
between those things which truly have large algorithmic randomness
("complexity"; as I said before, I think randomness is a better word) and
those for which it is only apparent.

Hal
