From: boojum!esr@gvls1.vfl.paramax.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: Discussion? Ritual -- yes.  Paganism -- no more.

> However to 1) put religious overtones on this (e.g., we summon the 
> spirit of the night woods) or 2) to claim that one needs the CORRECT
> pagan "slant" to get the full benefit is NON-Extropian.

I think you've mis-interpreted my claims slightly.  The mis-interpretation
is quite an understandable one, but it needs a serious refutation.  (Yes,
Max, this is going to be about ritual.)

What I do, mentally, when I put "religious" overtones on an experience like
dancing around a bonfire, is not the surrender to irrationalism that you seem
to assume.  Rather, it's a way of tuning or programming my brain to get
maximum benefits from the experience.

On the objective level, I don't believe in gods any more than I believe in the
tooth fairy or benign government.  On the *intersubjective* level, I and
co-ritualists may experience them as powerful transformative forces.

The question a true extropian needs to ask isn't the naive-rationalist "Which
reality-tunnel is *true*?" but the operational *what are they useful for?*.

We all know what the objective/scientific/operationalist head is good for.
But the intersubjective level has its uses, too. 

As for claiming that one needs the "CORRECT pagan slant", that's true, but
not in the way you obviously think.  It is not necessary to adopt any
particular theology to get maximum benefits from a bonfire dance.  It *is*
necessary to step out of normal waking consciousness to do so.

Bly's crowd isn't getting the full benefit because their mode-change technology
is timid and spotty.  Neopagan ritual inductions simply *work* better.  We've
had a lot more practice and we're not constrained to imagery that's
unthreatening to forty-year-old-white corporate executives.

Bly's people will probably evolve towards better methods over time.  As they do,
their ritual technology (though not necessarily their theology) will come to
resemble ours more, for the same reason all suspension bridges look basically
alike.
