Date: Sat, 10 Jul 93 14:11:12 PDT
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: AIT VirtSem: Determinism, Chaos, and These Ideas

Another interesting point raised by Tim is the idea of "found objects".
The Mandelbrot set is conceptually simple, but to actually describe a
very tiny piece of it may take a lot of information just to specify its
coordinates.  Simple equations do not necessarily imply that a resulting
object is simple.  This is related to the point someone made earlier
(quoting Gell-Mann?) that initial conditions may carry the bulk of the
information in most objects (as compared to the physics).

In AIT terms, one could trivially write a program which did "print all
strings".  If we wait long enough, every possible string will be output by
that program.  Does this imply that all strings have low AIT complexity,
since such a small program will output them?  No, because the question is
then WHERE in the huge output stream the particular string is found.  At
least as much information will be needed to specify this location than
would be needed just to output the string by an optimal AIT program.

This relates to a point made last year here in a post by Hans Moravec.
I didn't understand it very well, but he was arguing that the whole
universe could be created from nothingness.  The idea is that you could
create all possible universes more easily, in some sense, than to create
a particular universe.  It takes virtually no information.  Then each
universe evolves (with its own natural laws) and only those universes
where life develops are interesting.  By having this selection process
happen naturally rather than trying to carefully create one of the few
universes which can evolve life, God has an easier time of it.  (Well,
that wasn't quite what he said, but it's one way of looking at it, maybe...)

Another aspect of this point, which I touched on earlier, is the difference
between the Many-Worlds and standard Copenhagen interpretations of QM.
In MW, all possible events happen, which means that the total information
content is perhaps constant.  In Copenhagen, to describe the state of the
universe today you need to specify which particular quantum events have
occured in the past, so the information needed to describe the universe
constantly increases.  In MW terms, a single "Copenhagen" universe is just
part of the total universe state function, and focusing in one one of them
requires you to specify a lot of information just as focusing in on a tiny
part of the Mandelbrot set does.

It's kind of paradoxical that it takes less information to specify a
whole set than to specify just part of the set.  Well, maybe it makes
sense with the Mandelbrot set but it seems strange to imagine that a
Many-Worlds universe has less information than a Copenhagen universe.

Hal Finney              hfinney@shell.portal.com              HFINN on Hex

