Date: Sat, 10 Jul 93 17:41:55 -0700
From: drexler@netcom.com (K. Eric Drexler)
Subject: Technical Nanosystems criticisms

At 10:29 AM 7/10/93 -0700, hfinney@shell.portal.com wrote:
>My impression of James Donald's arguments about static friction...
>If the two rings are rigid, then the inner ring should rotate fairly freely...
>However, if the rings are flexible....The whole process of rotation, even very
>>slow rotation, will be a constant tug-and-snap, dissipating energy as
>vibrations of the rings.  There will be large amounts of static friction,
>even in the limit of slow rotation.

This is essentially correct, hence structures that resemble badly designed
bearings can be used as dampers and detents (Nanosystems p.314). Note,
however, that tug-and-snap isn't the issue in typical bearings, because the
interatomic forces are repulsive at close approach. Push-and-pop is a
better description Good bearings have been designed and analyzed using
standard molecular mechanics methods, and, of course, they don't do this.

>I may be misunderstanding James' arguments....

James' arguments indicated, without analysis, that (small) three-body
corrections to the attractive potential would change the qualitative
behavior of (all?) bearings. The class of dissipation mechanism you
identify is real, but arises from pairwise potentials. (The dominant role
of repulsive forces in determining friction further illustrates why small
corrections the attractive, long-range force component are of little
significance.)

-----------------------

>From the Preface to Nanosystems (p.xviii):

"_Criticism of criticism_"

"Research in molecular nanotechnology requires a design perspective because
it aims to describe workable systems. It is easy to describe unworkable
systems, and criticisms of a critic's own bad design have on occasion been
presented as if they were criticisms of molecular nanotechnology as a
whole."

-----------------------

Eric Drexler


