Date: Sun, 11 Jul 93 09:35:19 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: General Assemblers

I'm glad Derek Zahn is now able (as we all are again) to expand on his
earlier compressed post.  The concept of the general assembler goes to
the heart of my discomfort of the presentation of nanotechnology (along
with the overly mechanistic approach).

> I kind of hope that Mike is right -- the primary intuition behind
> self-rep as advancement is elegance.  A more complex, extended,
> system might be more pleasing.

Simple systems are more elegant and attractive, I agree.  But the
economy thrivse on complexity.  That's not to say that the simple,
elegant solutions are impossible, just that they tend to be out-competed
in the drive for lower costs, higher profits that the market requires.

>> Specialisation of function/division of labour strongly implies that
>> the reproductive and manufacturing functions should be separated.
>> Consider the ant.
> 
> Why?  Consider the polyp colonies that make up coral reefs.  Why is
> the ant a better metaphor than the vast majority of biological forms?

Because ant family (within the hymenoptera order of social insects) is
a very successful adaptation, having changed little over millions of
years, yet has filled many niches.  Also because they fascinate me :-)

> [..] if GAs are possible, they offer a potential for increased
> efficiency because the effort needed to effect a localized increase
> in production capacity involves only short-range transportation of
> matter.  The larger the production infrastructure, the further
> semi-assembled material has to be moved around, at great cost in
> failure modes and energy use.

That's not true in today's economy, why should it be true in tomorrow's? 
I note that the trend during the 20 century (except for the inter-war
years when growth was slow or negative) has been for the ratio of trade
/ output to rise with output and productivity levels.  This was probably
also true during the 19 century (but the data is fragmentary).  Trade
enable regions to exploit their comparative advantages.  I suggest that
the development of transportation infrastructures will continue to
reduce the cost of trade (relative to the final product), leading to
more and more products assembled from components manufactured across the
world.

The type of economy (in manufactures, at least) you envisage seems to
be a return to the locally sufficient communities that existed centuries
ago.  The local blacksmith working at his forge, local farms supplying
food.  And (almost) everybody living in grinding poverty.  One of the
reasons for the start of the industrial revolution (in the UK) was the
laying down of the canal transport infrastructure (for coal barges). 
Then came rail....

> Now, self-rep GAs [...] have their own efficiency penalties -- all
> based on whether they can be effective at both assembling product and
> other GAs effectively.  Because the structure of the devices they will
> construct will likely be very similar to themselves (little computers,
> diamondoid stuff, etc), my intuition tells me that if these kind of
> GAs can work, they would be a very effective (and even better -- very
> flexible!) production technology.

My intuition says the reverse.  Product complexity in manufacturing is
added a stage at a time, by assembly processes that are _more_ complex
than the complexity (value) they're adding.  The finished product may
still be more complex than any individual piece of machinery in the
assembly process.

I'm sure there will be a market for some type of GA, but I doubt they
will be zillions of wee little beasties all crawling over each other in
a vat.  More likely a GA will be a single integrated molecular machine
capable of constructing all objects below a certain complexity (and
probably not including itself).  Shovel in dirt and trace elements and
plans.  If you want more complex objects then either you _buy_ them or
upgrade your GA (just as we upgrade PCs today).

> Though note that Ralph Merkle and lately Drexler agrees with you,
> for the most part.  See _Unbounding the Future_, pp 140-141.

Yes, I noticed that.  Mentioned only in passing, as far as I could see. 
(Took Tim May's advice and skimmed it without purchase.  Couldn't see
much advance beyond EOC.).

> It might be possible to have the "wee little beasties" behave
> similar to coral; that is, always be in contact with one another.

How does that help?  Coral is hardly a very complex structure (that is
compared to the polyps).  As someone else said, if we're relying on some
type of diffusion process to localise the GAs it's difficult to see how
sharp precision manufacture is effected.

> If we can have equipment around the vat, it might be possible to
> communicate position and command electromagnetically from outside
> with phased-array antennas, but I don't know if the resolution
> would be good enough [...]

Sounds to me like you'd need a complexity of supporting command and
control structure greater than the finished product arrayed outside the
vat.  Why not go the whole hog and just construct the product outside?

> derek

Mike Price          price@price.demon.co.uk           AS member

