HEADLINE: Looking at What Monkeys Do

BODY: FORREST SAWYER: [voice-over] For years, scientists have 
argued whether it's human nature to be violently aggressive. Now 
meet what may be the human's closest relative, the  bonobo,  a 
primate society where the female leads, the male follows, and 
everyone has sex almost all the time.

FRANS de WAAL, Yerkes Primate Center: This is two juveniles who 
are doing oral sex.

SAWYER: [voice-over] And violence? It's virtually non-existent.  
Tonight, what might be learned when humans see what monkeys do.

ANNOUNCER: This is ABC News Nightline. Substituting for Ted 
Koppel and reporting from Washington, Forrest Sawyer.

SAWYER: Most of us picture ourselves, human beings, as special, 
unique in all the world. There are humans, and then, off in the 
distance somewhere, there's the rest of the animal kingdom. And, in 
many critical ways, that's true. No other animal has such 
extraordinary intelligence. No other animal can communicate so 
subtly, no other animal can shape its environment so completely. 
We're also filled with another trait, vast curiosity, which makes us 
think again, are we closer than we realize to our cousins, the apes? For 
years, scientists have studied our nearest relatives, the chimpanzees, 
an often violent, even murderous bunch, and speculated on what their 
behavior says about us. But now comes the  bonobo,  what some 
people call a pygmy chimpanzee, and a group of scientists whose new 
studies of this animal, known for decades, have convinced them there 
may be an entirely different message for us. So tonight, a mystery 
story, a search for a kind of holy grail, what it is that makes us 
uniquely human, and what it is that makes us part of some greater 
family. First, you should meet the  bonobos.  Here's Michael Guillen.

MICHAEL GUILLEN, ABC News: We don't tend to think of ourselves 
as being merely animals, but science does. According to zoologists, 
humans are primates, animals that typically have 3-D vision, hands 
and large brains. Now, for the longest time, scientists were willing to 
concede that, as primates go, humans were a breed apart. But now 
they're discovering some family resemblances that give a whole new 
meaning to the phrase "human animal."

FRANS de WAAL, Yerkes Primate Center: We are as close to the 
chimpanzee as a dog is to a fox, or a horse is to a zebra. I mean, that's 
fairly close.

GUILLEN: [voice- over] Genetically speaking, the DNA of humans 
and chimps are 98 percent identical. They're practically 
indistinguishable. That's why scientists have been studying 
chimpanzees for decades, looking for clues to our own behavior, 
especially in the areas of relationships, sexuality and conflict. Like 
humans, chimps can be extremely affectionate, but also extremely 
violent.

RICHARD WRANGHAM, Harvard University Anthropologist: Of 
the 100 or so primates that have been studied, only one shows the 
pattern of lethal raiding, in which male coalitions defend turf, and 
defend it very aggressively, even to the point of making attacks into 
the neighboring areas, and that one species of non-human primate 
that does that is the chimpanzee, that is to say, our closest relative.

GUILLEN: [voice-over] For years, chimpanzees have represented our 
best animal model 20for human behavior, but now that's changed 
dramatically, with the discovery of the  bonobos,  a primate that's 
genetically similar to chimps and humans, but whose behavior is 
radically different.

Mr. WRANGHAM: Chimpanzees and  bonobos  are so closely related 
to each other that it's arguable which is more closely related to 
humans. And yet you've got one species which shows this pattern of 
violence, and another, the  bonobo,  which shows extraordinary peace.

GUILLEN: [voice-over]  Bonobos  show none of the violent tendencies 
scientists have observed in chimps, thus sending into a tailspin the 
theory that violence is in our genes.  Bonobos  are also demolishing 
old theories about sexuality. Chimps have sex infrequently, and only 
for reproduction. By contrast,  bonobos  have sex several times a day, 
and furthermore, they are neither heterosexual nor homosexual.  They 
are pansexual, which means that males and females of all ages mate 
indiscriminately.

Dr. de WAAL: This is two juveniles who are doing oral sex, where 
one of them is between the legs of the other.

GUILLEN: How early in life do they start having sex?

Dr. de WAAL: Oh, at least, I would say, the age of one.

GUILLEN: [voice-over] Among primates in general, not just 
chimpanzees and  bonobos,  much of what we consider grossly illegal 
human behavior is not only legal, but natural. For example, under 
certain circumstances, gibbon families practice incest.

Mr. WRANGHAM: If one of the males or female dies, then if the 
offspring is the appropriate sex, they may stay in that group and mate 
with their parent.

GUILLEN: [voice- over] Among orangutans, males are much larger 
than females, and rape is the rule, not the exception, during sexual 
encounters. Among vervet monkeys, the problem is sexual 
harassment. About half the time, a female will rebuff a male's 
advances with a slap. But if there are primate counterparts to the Mr.  
Hyde in us, then there are counterparts to the Dr. Jekyll in us, as well.

ALISON RICHARD, Yale University Peabody Museum Director: I 
think that we can say that we all, as members of the order primates, 
have the capacity to form close social bonds.

GUILLEN: [voice-over] Chimpanzees nurse their young for four 
years. So do the  bonobos,  except there's an interesting twist. After 
the nurturing period, females leave to join another group, but males 
stay with their mothers for life. Mothers help their sons during fights, 
and in return, mothers, females in general, have a very high standing 
in  bonobo  society. [on camera] The similarities between us and other 
primates are important clues about our past, glimpses of how our 
ancestors might have lived millions of years ago. That's why scientists 
study them. But what do these animals tell us about our behavior 
today and, more importantly, what do they tell us about the 
possibility of changing that behavior for the better? [voice-over] 
Chimps teach us, or at least affirm, that to err is primate, to forgive 
divine. After fighting with one another, hostile chimps will often kiss 
and hug to make up.  Bonobos,  too, teach us a lesson, a rather 
surprising one. Their X-rated behavior, it turns out, is their way of 
maintaining their G-rated serenity. We saw a dramatic illustration of 
this at the San Diego Zoo, home to the largest captive population of  
bonobos  in the United States. Zoology student Amy Parrish 
explained why the  bonobos  suddenly started having sex, as soon as 
they saw food.

AMY PARRISH: In the wild, when animals encounter food, there's a 
lot of aggression and competition over the food, and instead, in  
bonobos,  what you see is, they engage in sex first, and that appears to 
be a tension-reduction function.

GUILLEN: [voice- over] Here, for example, a fight between two 
females immediately turns into a sexual encounter. To scientists, the 
lesson from all this is not that promiscuity leads to peacefulness. The 
lesson, they say, is that if  bonobos  have found a way to overcome 
their aggressions, then maybe there's hope for their more 
sophisticated relative, Homo sapiens sapien.

Mr. WRANGHAM: Clearly, people have the capacity to be nasty to 
each other with extraordinary regularity over time and space. But the 
fact that it's in our nature doesn't mean that it has to inevitably 
emerge.

Dr. de WAAL: So if you don't teach your children to act in a particular 
way, just because you think it's all right that they act like "animals," as 
we call it, that's your responsibility.

GUILLEN: [voice-over] Our society, de Waal says, can push us in 
different directions. Some societies are violent; others are peaceful. 
We have options, and the responsibility to select those options that 
bring out the animal, the good animal, in us all. For Nightline, I'm 
Michael Guillen in San Diego.

SAWYER: And, when we come back, we'll be joined by our  bonobo  
expert, Dr. de Waal, and by an expert in the behavior of another 
primate, humans.

[Commercial break]

SAWYER: Frans de Waal is a research professor at the Yerkes Primate 
Research Center at Emory University.  He is a  bonobo  expert and the 
author of Peacemaking Among Primates, which traces his 15 years of 
primate study. He joins us from our Atlanta bureau.  Lionel Tiger is 
the Charles Darwin professor of anthropology at Rutgers University. 
He wrote The Imperial Animal, and a lot of other books, about how 
human drive and emotion vary among cultures. Dr. Tiger joins us in 
our New York studios. Dr. de Waal, these are fascinating studies you 
have done of the  bonobos,  or the pygmy chimpanzees, as some 
people call them, but I guess a lot of people would feel it's a bit of a 
stretch to go from those critters to say that there's a lot you can learn 
about human beings from them. Can you help us understand why 
you can make that conclusion?

FRANS de WAAL, Emory University: No, you wouldn't consider it a 
stretch if we were to compare rhesus monkeys with chimpanzees, and 
rhesus monkeys are much more different from chimpanzees than we 
are from them. So we are very close to chimpanzees, and in that 
regard, it's not a stretch at all.

SAWYER: We're very close, not only in terms of our genetic base, but 
we're very close behaviorly?

Dr. de WAAL: Yeah, yeah. There's more and more indications.  
Almost every behavior that you could mention, except maybe 
language, that's maybe a problem area for comparison, but almost all 
social behavior, you can draw parallels.

SAWYER: But you see, right away we want to say, "Hold on a second, 
we're really lots more complicated than them. They are fairly 
primitive creatures. They can't have all those sets of behaviors.

Dr. de WAAL: Well, primitive is maybe not the word you would use.

SAWYER: But you see, there are a lot of folks who are going to say 
that, they're going to say they're much more simple than we are, they 
can't have all the sets of complicated social behaviors and ways of 
interacting that we do.

Dr. de WAAL: No, I think it is simpler, their societies are simpler than 
ours, there's no denying that. But, for example, in chimpanzees you 
find simple political structures. Chimpanzees form coalitions and the 
power of a male chimpanzee is based on who supports him, not on 
physical strength. So as soon as you get a system like that, you get 
strategies that are very different than strategies that are based on 
physical strength. For example, male chimpanzees, just like our 
politicians nowadays, they go around and play with female's babies in 
order to be popular among females, because they need the support of 
females in their power struggles. So you get a political system that 
starts looking like a human system.

SAWYER: Well, Dr. Tiger, you come at this from another direction. 
You start by studying the human animal. Do these studies provide 
some insight to you?

LIONEL TIGER, Rutgers University: Well, they're exciting and novel 
and certainly they stimulate the imagination.  It's a question whether 
you can look at one species of primate, say the large chimps, and then 
look at another, the small chimps, and say one or another is a better 
model of who we are. But we are who we are, we are a species, we 
don't breed with those other animals, that's how you define a species, 
and I think that, notwithstanding the fact that we get a tremendous 
amount of emotional and intellectual and even aesthetic appreciation 
of life from the other primates, I think we also have to keep a very 
firm focus, a humble focus, on our own species. And to do that means 
to appreciate that we are evolved, we separated from those other 
chimps at certain points, and we developed our own way of life and, 
while again, it's exciting to look at these other animals, and I think it's 
one of the most worthwhile things scientists can do, nevertheless, we 
have to keep a still, clear eye on ourselves.

SAWYER: But is there something that we can learn about the very 
basis, the foundation of the way that we organize ourselves, by 
looking at these, what Dr. de Waal says are simpler societies, but are, 
in fact, societies?

Dr. TIGER: Well, let's take this whole question of male coalitions. I 
was very interested, for example, in the success - and this is not a 
political statement - but in the success of the bus tour, that now famed 
bus tour that Clinton and Gore took. I think one of the reasons that 
people found this interesting was that here you had two relatively 
aggressive, assertive males cooperating in a very attractive way. And 
that sense of effective coalitions, or an effective coalition, has a lot of 
resonance for any primate, I think, and certainly it appears to have 
had some resonance in that situation. So I think, without saying that 
we're as simple as those other animals, it's rather like a language. 
They have vowels and consonants; the end up being simpler in their 
usage than our language, but it's still vowels and consonants we're 
talking about, it's still coalitions, it's still affection, it's still being 
irascible or irritated, it's still being maternal or paternal. So we can 
see, in the language of behavior, if you will, the constituents of those 
other animals' behavior are very much like our behavior. But again, as 
Frans said, we're more complex, but we're more complex, I think, 
using many of the same simple features.

SAWYER: Dr. de Waal, I know that you have a fascination with the 
behavior of primates and monkeys, for that matter, of itself. Don't you 
find it interesting that the rest of us want to look over at your studies 
and say, "Well, golly, what does that say about us?"

Dr. de WAAL: Yes. I started as a zoologist, and zoologists study 
animals, basically, because of an interest in animals. But if you study 
animals that are so close with you, you automatically start comparing 
them with ourselves. And so I don't see that as a negative point. If I 
would be studying ants, maybe, the comparisons would be less 
obvious than when you study chimpanzees.

SAWYER: Go ahead.

Dr. de WAAL: The thing that I agree with Lionel Tiger about, the 
human species being a unique species, and I feel that if you want to 
understand humans, you study humans. That's the first thing you will 
do. But it is true that if you want to understand where certain 
behaviors or certain structures in our society come from in the past, 
you can go back to our relatives, and the more relatives share certain 
characteristics with us, the older the characteristic usually is.

SAWYER: And one of those traits that you're saying is the way we 
organize ourselves, the kinds of complicated power relationships that 
we see in our offices every day, or at the factory every day or, for that 
matter, at home, with the families every day, starts way back there?

Dr. de WAAL: Yeah. For example, if you look at peacemaking 
strategies, what animals do after fights, chimpanzees kiss and 
embrace after fights,  bonobos  usually have sex after fights. So you 
have a process that we call reconciliation, and I've talked quite a few 
times with family therapists about the process of reconciliation, and 
obviously, in humans there are many other things possible, for 
example, there are verbal reconciliations possible which are not 
common in the primates, obviously. But there are strong parallels 
there.

SAWYER: Well, this business about sex seems a pretty good place to 
take a pause. We'll come back and talk about that in just one 
moment.[Family Tree: 7 million years ago, one primate ancestry; 
today: gorilla, chimp,  bonobo,  human]

[Commercial break]

SAWYER: We're talking with Frans de Waal and with Lionel Tiger, 
and the subject is what we can learn from our nearest relatives, the 
primates and apes. Dr. Tiger, sex is an intriguing subject when we 
look over at the  bonobos.  Seems like they've figured out a way to 
keep peace, and I suppose most marriages would say sex is pretty 
good at keeping peace, too.

Dr. TIGER: Well, yes, except that I think if we look again at the human 
species, as Frans de Waal agreed we should earlier, we find that, in 
general, in most human societies, particularly in the large ones, sex is 
never something that's used solely for reconciliation. It may be in a 
pre-existing group, say, in a married pair or a pair of lovers. But it 
appears that humans have a much surlier approach to the whole 
question of who can have sex, when they can have it, with whom they 
can have it, and what the consequences are. We have only to look, for 
example, at the extraordinary power of organized religion to 
recognize that one of the things that these organizations really 
specialize in is controlling sexual behavior. And I think it's as 
interesting to ask why do they want to control our sexual behavior as 
to ask why do we want to have sexual behavior, and I think that's one 
of the kind of interesting puzzles that allows us to make intriguing 
comparisons between ourselves and the other primates.

SAWYER: Well, you can't open that door without walking through it. 
Why is there such a powerful urge to control sexual behavior among 
humans?

Dr. TIGER: Well, I published in January a book called The Pursuit of 
Pleasure, in which I try to address the whole question of why is it that 
we're so concerned about other people's pleasure. Why does 
somebody tell me I can't watch a particular movie, or read a particular 
book, or see a particular painting or photograph? And I suspect it's 
because in the kind of human systems we have created since the 
agricultural revolution, where we have thousands and thousands of 
people living together - and remember, most of the organized 
religions came at the time of the agricultural revolution, about 8,000 
years ago - at that time, suddenly sex became not a reconciliation 
possibility, but an explosive possibility. And if we look at what those 
early religions started to say, it was essentially "Thou shalt not." And I 
think that there's a clue there to the kind of historical or prehistorical 
imbalance that we have as a species between where we evolved, 
which is where the chimps still live and the  bonobos  still live, and 
where we now live, which is in cities, in large-scale communities. And 
I think that we have to acknowledge that we're not like those other 
animals now, because we're far, far removed from the environment in 
which we evolved and to which we specialized.

SAWYER: Dr. de Waal, when we talk about sex among the  bonobos,  
they don't seem to have too many limits, am I right?

Dr. de WAAL: No, sex in  bonobos  is awfully casual. They have sex in 
all combinations, in all positions that you can imagine, and even in 
many positions that are very hard to imagine, and they use sex to 
relax themselves and they also use sex to avoid problems among 
themselves. And I sometimes have this impression, and I would 
agree, I think, with what Lionel Tiger is saying, that maybe our 
ancestors were like that, maybe they did use sex as a sort of "make-
love-not-war" type of strategy, and that in the course of time, as a 
development of the nuclear family, sex became more and more 
limited and restricted and controlled in our societies, and the things 
that we still have today, such as pedophilia and homosexuality and a 
lot of other sexual orientations, that they are sort of leftovers of that 
earlier period in which sex was much more widely and much more 
publicly used in the society.

SAWYER: Dr. de Waal, let's talk for a moment about violence. I 
remember back when I was in college, there was all this talk about the 
idea that humans somehow became humans because they were killer 
apes, they were violent, they were murderous, and they went around 
wiping out other folks and thus became more intelligent somehow. 
That seems not to be so.

Dr. de WAAL: Yeah. I think that's a very one-sided picture of the 
human species, as it is also a very one-sided picture of the 
chimpanzee, to say it's a very violent species. Because the human 
species and the chimpanzee, and the  bonobo,  they have very strong 
checks and balances on aggression, and so aggressive behavior 
evolved together with those checks and balances. And I sometimes 
have discussions with child psychologists who argue that it would be 
good to educate children to reduce aggression levels. I think the main 
problem is not to reduce aggression levels. The main problem is to 
enhance the mechanisms that we have to control aggression, and-

Dr. TIGER: Let- yeah, I just want to jump in here, simply because 
again, you know, we all agree on the flexibility of human behavior, 
but it seems to me that we've had enough examples of, say, in 
Yugoslavia, three generations, three and a half generations of people 
who are- who have been trained to live together under the Marxist 
system, and suddenly that cap is removed and we begin to find some 
extraordinarily deep antipathies, violences which are heartbreaking to 
watch on television, for example, and for the people who are there. 
And so I think that there's a need to be as willing to accept that we 
have peaceful strategies as we have to be wary of and attentive to the 
possibility that we have aggressive strategy, because Lord knows, 
whenever we pick up the newspaper or turn on the tube, somebody's 
killing somebody.

Dr. de WAAL: Oh, yeah, I know.  Yeah.

Dr. TIGER: And usually it's for reasons which, to the outsider, seem 
peculiar.

Dr. de WAAL: Yeah. No, it's for sure that maybe for a long time 
people have been naive about themselves, but for the last 25 years, 
biology books have emphasized the aggressive nature of humans, and 
I think it is about time we also started looking at the other side of 
human nature. And obviously, there's plenty of evidence for violence 
in humans, there's no doubt about it. It's a very violent species.

SAWYER: Dr. Tiger, I hate to do this to you, but I've got about 15 
seconds. Is there something special we learn about humans by 
engaging in these primate studies?

Dr. TIGER: One of the main things we learn is a kind of deep humility 
about our role in nature. If we understood monkeys and apes and 
other animals better, I think we'd have been attentive more explicitly 
and quickly to the environment and to the other issues of our 
relationship to nature. Not only that, we'd become more attentive to 
our own relationship to our own nature, and I think that's absolutely 
critical.

SAWYER: Thank you for a fascinating conversation. We could talk for 
hours, but time has caught us. We'll be back in just one moment.

[Commercial break]

SAWYER: That is our report for tonight.  I'm Forrest Sawyer in 
Washington. For all of us here at ABC News, good night.

